  [plan] action=answer paper='' queries=[]
         reasoning='We have excerpts for every named paper that discusses program synthesis, so no further search is needed. The papers mostly agree on synthesis as generating a program/implementation from a specification, but disagree on what counts as the specification and how the search space is constrained:\n\n- Pu et al. (ICML 2018): synthesis is a regression problem that seeks an exact-fit source-code program mapping inputs to outputs; it is commonly formulated as constraint satisfaction from input-output examples, with a DSL plus specification.\n- Solar-Lezama (Sketch, APLAS 2009): synthesis is localized/partial-program completion; the programmer supplies a sketch and either tests or a reference implementation, and the synthesizer fills in the low-level details.\n- Singh et al. (storyboards, SIGSOFT 2011): synthesis is constraint-based search over a parameterized program representing the solution space.\n- Inala et al. (recursive ADT transformations, 2015): synthesis takes correctness specifications plus reusable components/templates, then searches among programs built from those components.\n- Alur et al. (SyGuS-Comp 2016/2017): a SyGuS instance is a logical specification φ plus a grammar G; a solution is a function expressible in G that makes φ valid after substitution.\n- Fisman et al. (FMSD 2021): program synthesis is deriving an executable implementation from a logical specification; syntax-guided synthesis adds syntactic constraints to limit the search space.\n- Singh et al. (PVLDB 2017): partial programs with holes, grammars, and behavioral constraints from examples; additionally they allow an optimization variant because not all examples can be satisfied.\n- Ellis et al. (NIPS 2018): synthesis is an optimization problem over a DSL, with a specification and optionally a cost function; they seek minimum-cost programs consistent with the spec.\n\nWhere they disagree:\n1) Specification type: examples/tests vs logical formulas vs reference implementations vs mixed/behavioral constraints.\n2) Search restriction: unconstrained program search vs partial programs vs grammars/DSLs vs reusable components/templates.\n3) Objective: exact satisfaction vs minimum-cost/optimization (explicit in Ellis 2018 and PVLDB 2017’s optimization variant).\n4) Problem framing: some call synthesis a regression problem (Pu 2018), while others frame it as logical derivation or constraint solving (SyGuS, FMSD, Sketch).\n\nSo the definitions are compatible at a high level but differ on the formal interface supplied to the synthesizer and whether the task is pure feasibility or optimization.'
